 2. ✈️ Axios interview: Haberman on Trump&#x27;s ruse 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 The catering truck used to secretly move President Trump from Air Force One to another plane following the NATO summit last month in Ankara, Turkey. Photo: Alex Brandon/AP New York Times reporter Maggie Haberman tells me in a "Behind the Curtain" interview — out tomorrow — that President Trump's cloak-and-dagger exit from last month's NATO summit in Turkey offers a revealing glimpse into how his White House operates.

 "Donald Trump is very secretive — very secretive," says Haberman, co-author with Jonathan Swan of " Regime Change ," which has spent six weeks at No. 1 on the New York Times bestseller list. "He does not like letting information out that he has not authorized." Trump's second administration is "very, very good at keeping secrets when it wants to," Haberman added. "They value secrecy above the need for transparency of any kind with the general public." Between the lines: Haberman says Trump's willingness to be spirited away in a catering container shows how serious the concerns about his safety were.

 "For him, frankly, to be willing to even get in a cart like that, which I imagine didn't smell good and wasn't especially clean, speaks to the level of concern about his safety," she said. ▶️ Watch : 2-min. clip ... Subscribe to our YouTube channel . 

 President Trump discusses his secret flight last night. Photo: Anna Moneymaker/Getty Images Trump told reporters last night after stepping off Air Force One at Joint Base Andrews, Md., that the plane switch was "up to Secret Service. I just follow what they'd like to do. So I go by Secret Service — and the military. They wanted me to go in a different flight, a different plane — equal safety. But they wanted me to do it, so I do it. I do what they say. ... I guess there was a threat out there. I didn't really ask too much about it. I get a lot of threats."

