 3. 👀 AOC&#x27;s &quot;Woke 1&quot; defense 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Jonathan Karl interviews Rep. Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez on Sunday on ABC's "This Week." Photo: Pawel Kaminski/ABC Rep. Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez (D-N.Y.) is trying to pull off a political feat for the ages: bury a chapter of left-wing politics that seemed like a good idea to many liberals at the time — but not so much anymore, Axios' Mike Zapler writes. 

 Why it matters: The country's most prominent democratic socialist is essentially seeking a mulligan for candidates who embraced policies like defunding the police, arguing that candidates should be judged by their current platforms. But that'll be a tough sell with moderate Democrats, let alone Republicans, in competitive races outside of blue bastions. 📺 Zoom in: In an interview with Jonathan Karl on ABC's "This Week" on Sunday, AOC was asked about some of the positions democratic socialists took around 2020. They were, she said, a product of the time.

 "Woke 1 was crazy," she laughed, quoting a politician-friend . "And I think that what's important is that we have to assess what a candidate is saying now." Between the lines: AOC is arguing for a fresh look at today's candidates. But plenty of Democrats rejected those positions in real time.

 That list was topped by Joe Biden, who went on to win the primary and then the presidency on a center-left platform. 🔭 Zoom out: AOC's "Woke 1 was crazy" explanation looks like an attempt to redefine democratic socialism — distancing it from cultural and social positions that are hard to defend now and moving toward a populist economic agenda with broader appeal.

 Her own political future — whether it's a Senate run or a presidential campaign in 2028 — could be the ultimate test of that effort. Share this story . 

 
 
