 6. 🔬 Exclusive: AI&#x27;s cancer trial boost 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Stock: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 AI is starting to unlock millions of dollars of new efficiencies in clinical trials on cancer treatments, Axios' Adriel Bettelheim writes from a new Tufts study. 

 Why it matters: AI-powered tools could shave months off time-consuming processes like recruiting and enrolling patients, monitoring results and interpreting data. 🧪 What they found: The analysis, from the Tufts Center for the Study of Drug Development, found AI agents can accelerate clinical development of cancer drugs by approximately 10 weeks and reduce direct operating costs by as much as $5.6 million in late-stage trials.

 The efficiencies increase with the number of tumors a drug can target: An experimental treatment with 50 active uses could see benefits as high as $565 million. Keep reading ... Get Axios Vitals . 

 
 
